.TH CUT 1 ""
.SH NAME
paste \- merge same lines of several files or subsequent lines of one file
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpaste file1 file2 ...
.br
\fBpaste -d\fPlist file1 file2 ...
.br
\fBpaste -s [-d]\fPlist file1 file2 ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
In the first two forms, \fIpaste\fP concatenates corresponding lines
of the given input files \fIfile1, file2,\fP etc.  It treats each file
as a column or columns of a table and pastes them together
horizontally (parallel merging).  If you will, it is the counterpart
of \fIcat\fP(1) which concatenates vertically, i.e., one file after
the other. In the last form above, \fIpaste\fP replaces the function
of an older command with the same name by combining subsequent lines
of the input file (serial merging).  In all cases, lines are glued
together with the \fItab\fP character, or with characters from an
optionally specified \fIlist\fP.  Output is to the standard output, so
it can be used as the start of a pipe, or as a filter, if \fB\-\fP is
used in place of a file name.
.PP
The meanings of the options are:
.TP .75
\fB-d\fP
Without this option, the new-line characters of each but the last file
(or last line in case of the \fB\-s\fP option) are replaced by a
\fItab\fP character.  This option allows replacing the \fItab\fP
character by one or more alternate characters (see below).
.TP
\fIlist\fP
One or more characters immediately following \fB\-d\fP replace the
default \fItab\fP as the line concatenation character.  The list is
used circularly, i.e., when exhausted, it is reused.  In parallel
merging (i.e., no \fB\-s\fP option) the lines from the last file are
always terminated with a new-line character, not from the \fIlist\fP.
The list may contain the special escape sequences: \fB\\n\fP
(new-line), \fB\\t\fP (tab), \fB\\\\\fP (backslash), and \fB\\0\fP
(empty string, not a null character.)  Quoting may be necessary, if
characters have special meaning to the shell (e.g., to get one
backslash, use \fI\-d"\\\\\\\\"\fP).  SPECIAL NOTE: This version of
\fIpaste\fP has had the list of special characters expanded from the
Unix(Tm) standard to include the following additional characters:
\fB\\b\fP (backspace), \fB\\f\fP (form-feed), \fB\\r\fP (carriage
return), \fB\\v\fP (vertical tab).
.TP
\fB\-s\fP
Merge subsequent lines rather than one from each input file.  Use
\fItab\fP for concatenation, unless a \fIlist\fP is specified with
\fB\-d\fP option.  Regardless of the \fIlist\fP, the very last
character of the file is forced to be a new-line.
.TP
\fB\-\fP
May be used in place of any file name, to read a line from the
standard input. (There is no prompting).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP 2.25
ls | paste \-d\" \" -
list directory in one column
.TP
ls | paste \- \- \- \-
list directory in four columns
.SH SEE ALSO
grep(1),paste(1).
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP 2.0
\fIno delimiters\fP
Missing \fIlist\fP for \fB\-d\fP option
.TP
\fI<filename> : cannot open\fP
\fIpaste\fP was given a bad filename
.TP
\fItoo many files\fP
Except for \fB\-s\fP option, no more than 12 input files may be
specified.
.SH CAVEATS
This program is a complete rewrite of the Bell Laboratories command of
the same name; no part of the original source or manual is included.
Therefore, you may feel free to use it, and its source, without violation
of \fPany\fP contract agreements.  However, I retain the copyright in order to
specify it remain available for use by all and sundry, without
cost.  Feel free to modify as necessary, although I went to great
pains to recreate the behavior of the original command; I would suggest
this congruence be maintained.
.PP
Along the same lines, although I've made a reasonable effort to test
the more arcane behavior of the original \fIpaste\fP and reproduce it,
there are no guarantees; and there \fIare\fP four known differences
between the original program and documentation, and this version.  All
are downward compatible.  First, \fIpaste\fP as distributed with Unix
has a bug in the way it handles multiple files for the \fB\-s\fP
option.  This has been fixed, and the bug reported to the providers of
the Unix command.  Secondly, the list of valid escape characters for
the \fIlist\fP has been expanded.  Third, the 511 character/line
restriction has been removed.  Finally, extant undocumented diagnostics
have been added to the man page, and the one pertaining to the (now
obsolete) \fIline too long\fP removed.
.PP
